<header><p class="st-kicker">other · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">East Hebei Autonomous Government</h1><p class="st-meta">1935 CE – 1938 CE · capital Tongzhou</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Japanese-sponsored puppet administration in eastern Hebei that served as a channel for smuggling and as a step towards Japan's war on China.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tanggu-truce">Tanggu Truce</a> of 1933 created a demilitarised zone south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-wall-of-china">Great Wall</a>. In November 1935, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> pressed for the separation of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, the local administrator <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yin-rugeng">Yin Rugeng</a> declared the twenty-two counties of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebei">Hebei</a> autonomous from the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/nationalist-government">Nationalist government</a>, with its seat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tongzhou-district">Tongzhou</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The administration was dependent on the Japanese army. It charged its own low customs rates, which allowed Japanese goods, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> and narcotics to flood into northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, undermining the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/nationalist-government">Nationalist government</a>'s revenues. Its peace preservation corps provided local security.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In July 1937, soon after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lugou-bridge">Marco Polo Bridge</a> incident, its own Chinese security troops mutinied at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tongzhou-district">Tongzhou</a> and killed many Japanese and Korean residents. The regime was absorbed into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/provisional-government-of-the-republic-of-china">Provisional Government of the Republic of China</a> in February 1938.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/east-hebei-autonomous-government.jpg" alt="East Hebei Autonomous Government" width="320" height="201"><figcaption>Image: Original: en:Provisional Government of the Republic of China (1912) Vector: Kibinsky · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_China_(1912%E2%80%931928).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1935 CE – 1938 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Tongzhou</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Yin Rugeng</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Chinese folk religion, Buddhism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Mandarin</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>East Hopei, Jidong</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1936&amp;focus=east-hebei-autonomous-government" aria-label="East Hebei Autonomous Government on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7559 753 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where East Hebei Autonomous Government lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7879 1009L8529 1145M7879 1009L7868 1006"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M8529 1145h0M7868 1006h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7879 1009h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7879 1009h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1936&amp;focus=east-hebei-autonomous-government">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
